Computational Synthesis for Simultaneous Stabilization via Convex Relaxations and Learning-Based Warm-Starting

Ouadi Maakoul

A thesis submitted in partial fulfilment of the requirements for the degree of Doctor of Philosophy in Control Theory and Optimization

---

Abstract

The problem of simultaneous stabilization—finding a single linear time-invariant controller that internally stabilizes a given finite family of plants—has been a central open question in systems and control theory for more than four decades. For two plants the answer is complete: it reduces to strong stabilization and is decided by a real-axis parity interlacing property (PIP). For three or more plants, however, the picture changes fundamentally. Seminal work by Blondel, Gevers, Mortini, and Rupp (1994) demonstrated that there exist triples of strictly proper SISO plants that are pairwise simultaneously stabilizable yet cannot be stabilized by any single controller. The obstruction is topological in nature—it involves incompatible winding numbers in the complex plane that have no signature on the real axis—and the decision problem is known to be NP-hard.

This thesis develops a computational synthesis framework that attacks simultaneous stabilization through a combination of convex relaxations and learning-based initialization. Using the Youla–Kučera parameterization, we recast the problem as the existence of a stable free parameter $Q(s)\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ satisfying unit conditions for each plant other than the nominal one. By restricting $Q$ to a fixed McMillan degree, these conditions are transformed, via the Routh–Hurwitz criterion, into a finite set of polynomial inequalities in the real coefficients of $Q$. The resulting polynomial feasibility problem is embedded into Lasserre’s semidefinite programming (SDP) hierarchy, which provides a sequence of convex relaxations that is asymptotically complete: if a simultaneously stabilizing controller of bounded complexity exists, a certificate of feasibility is guaranteed to be found at a finite relaxation order.

To overcome the computational cost of high-order relaxations, we introduce a novel learning-based warm-start strategy. A neural network—the Youla Prediction Network (YPN)—is trained offline on a distribution of solved plant triples to predict promising Youla parameter coefficients. The prediction is then refined by the SOS hierarchy, yielding dramatic speed-ups while preserving all rigorous guarantees. The YPN architecture, a feasibility-aware surrogate loss, and the integration with the SDP hierarchy are presented and proved correct. Generalization bounds based on Rademacher complexity guarantee that the prediction error remains small on unseen instances. The thesis also provides a self-contained dissection of the Blondel counterexample, delineates the complexity-theoretic landscape including NP-hardness and tractable subclasses, and outlines the design of the open-source SIMSTAB toolbox.

The proposed methodology offers the first systematic computational pipeline for simultaneous stabilization that is both practically efficient and mathematically certified, bridging the gap between theoretical intractability and engineering needs.

