1.6 A Note on Collaboration and Scope

This project is envisioned as a joint effort within our research group, building on preliminary formulations and the observation that “no simple algebraic synthesis works in general” for $n\ge 3$. The present thesis focuses exclusively on the theoretical and algorithmic foundations; experimental validation through physical tests or code training lies beyond the current scope and is left as future work. The numerical illustrations are designed to exemplify the expected behavior of the proposed algorithms and are not the result of executed simulations.

We are convinced that the time is ripe to revisit simultaneous stabilization not as a purely algebraic puzzle, but as a tractable computational challenge. The tools of the past decade—scalable SOS, deep learning for control, and robust convex programming—give us reason to believe that a practical solution is within reach, even if the theoretical worst-case remains hard.

Chapter 2: Mathematical Background

This chapter establishes the algebraic and analytic foundations upon which the remainder of the thesis rests. We work exclusively in the frequency domain with real‑rational transfer functions. The primary algebraic object is the ring of stable proper transfer functions, $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$. Coprime factorizations over this ring provide the framework for describing all stabilizing controllers for a single plant—the celebrated Youla–Kučera parameterization. We then apply this machinery to the simultaneous stabilization problem. For two plants the problem is equivalent to strong stabilization and is decided by the parity interlacing property (PIP) on the real axis. For three or more plants, however, the PIP is no longer sufficient; a topological obstruction in the complex plane, first exhibited by Blondel, Gevers, Mortini, and Rupp (1994), proves that no finite set of algebraic conditions can serve as a necessary and sufficient test. The chapter concludes by formulating the simultaneous stabilization condition as a semi‑algebraic feasibility problem and by outlining the sum‑of‑squares (SOS) tools that will be used to solve it.

