2.1 The Ring $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$

Definition 2.1 (Stable proper transfer functions).
$\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ is the set of all real‑rational transfer functions $G(s)$ that are proper (i.e., bounded at infinity) and analytic in the closed right half‑plane $\overline{\mathbb{C}}^+ = \{s\in\mathbb{C} : \operatorname{Re}(s)\ge 0\}$. Equivalently, $G(s)$ has no poles with non‑negative real part.
Under pointwise addition and multiplication, $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ is a commutative Banach algebra with identity when equipped with the $\mathcal{H}_\infty$ norm

\|G\|_\infty = \sup_{\operatorname{Re}(s)\ge 0}|G(s)| = \sup_{\omega\in\mathbb{R}}|G(j\omega)|.

The second equality follows from the maximum modulus principle.
A transfer function $U\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ is called a unit if its inverse $1/U$ also belongs to $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$. This occurs precisely when $U$ has no zeros in $\overline{\mathbb{C}}^+$ and $U(\infty)\neq 0$. The set of all units in $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ is denoted $\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{RH}_\infty)$.
Convention: in this thesis all transfer functions are assumed causal, i.e., proper, and all stabilising controllers are proper.

Remark 2.1. Working over $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ embeds stability directly into the algebra: a controller $C\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ is by definition stable.

---

2.2 Coprime Factorizations over $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$

Let $P(s)$ be a real‑rational proper transfer function. The plant may possess poles in $\overline{\mathbb{C}}^+$; we need a stable representation of $P$.

Definition 2.2 (Right and left coprime factorizations).
A pair $(N,D)$ with $N,D\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ is a right coprime factorization (RCF) of $P$ if $P = N D^{-1}$ and there exist $X,Y\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ satisfying the Bézout identity

X N + Y D = 1. \tag{2.1}

A pair $(\tilde D,\tilde N)$ with $\tilde D,\tilde N\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ is a left coprime factorization (LCF) if $P = \tilde D^{-1}\tilde N$ and there exist $\tilde X,\tilde Y\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ such that

\tilde N \tilde X + \tilde D \tilde Y = 1. \tag{2.2}

Definition 2.3 (Doubly coprime factorization).
A collection of eight matrices $N,D,\tilde N,\tilde D,X,Y,\tilde X,\tilde Y\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ is called a doubly coprime factorization (DCF) of $P$ if $P = N D^{-1} = \tilde D^{-1}\tilde N$ and the Bézout matrix identity holds:

\begin{pmatrix} \tilde X & -\tilde Y \\ -\tilde N & \tilde D \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} D & Y \\ N & X \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} I & 0 \\ 0 & I \end{pmatrix}. \tag{2.3}

Proposition 2.1. Every real‑rational proper transfer function admits a doubly coprime factorization.

Proof sketch. Write $P = n(s)/d(s)$ with $n,d$ coprime polynomials, $\deg n \le \deg d$. Choose a Hurwitz polynomial $\Lambda(s)$ of degree equal to $\max(\deg n,\deg d)$. Set $N = n/\Lambda$, $D = d/\Lambda$; both belong to $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$. From the Euclidean algorithm there exist polynomials $x(s),y(s)$ with $x n + y d = 1$. Dividing by $\Lambda$ gives $X = x/\Lambda$, $Y = y/\Lambda$ in $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ and (2.1). The left factors are obtained analogously. ∎

Thus every plant can be stably represented, and the Bézout identity ensures that the representation captures all unstable dynamics.

---

2.3 Internal Stability of a Feedback Loop

Consider the standard unity‑feedback interconnection of plant $P$ and controller $C$, with exogenous inputs $r$ (reference) and $d$ (output disturbance), and internal signals $e = r - y$, $u = C e$. The closed‑loop transfer functions from $(r,d)$ to $(e,u)$ are

\begin{aligned}
H_{er} &= (I+PC)^{-1}, & H_{ed} &= -(I+PC)^{-1}P,\\
H_{ur} &= C(I+PC)^{-1}, & H_{ud} &= -C(I+PC)^{-1}P.
\end{aligned}

Definition 2.4 (Internal stability). The feedback system is internally stable if all four transfer functions $H_{er}, H_{ed}, H_{ur}, H_{ud}$ belong to $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$.

We work with factorizations. Let $P = N D^{-1}$ be an RCF and suppose $C$ is expressed as a ratio of two stable matrices, $C = X_c D_c^{-1}$, with $X_c,D_c\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ and $D_c$ not identically zero.

Theorem 2.1 (Stability via factorizations).
The closed‑loop is internally stable if and only if the characteristic function

\Delta(s) := D_c(s) D(s) + X_c(s) N(s)

is a unit in $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$.

Proof. Computing the four maps yields rational expressions with denominator $\Delta$. Post‑multiplying by $D_c^{-1}$ etc., one finds that all four are in $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ exactly when $\Delta^{-1}\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$, i.e., $\Delta\in\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{RH}_\infty)$. ∎

This theorem is the bridge between coprime factorizations and stability.

---

2.4 The Youla–Kučera Parameterization

Discovered independently by Youla, Bongiorno, and Lu (1976) and Kučera (1975), this result parameterizes all controllers that internally stabilize a given plant.

Theorem 2.2 (Youla–Kučera).
Let $P$ possess a DCF $(N,D,\tilde N,\tilde D,X,Y,\tilde X,\tilde Y)$ over $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$. The set of all controllers that internally stabilize $P$ is

\mathcal{C}(P) = \left\{ C_Q = (X + D Q)(Y - N Q)^{-1} \;:\; Q\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty,\; \det(Y-N Q)(\infty)\neq 0 \right\}. \tag{2.4}

Equivalently, $C_Q = (\tilde X + Q \tilde D)^{-1}(\tilde Y - Q \tilde N)$ using the left factors.

Proof.
(Sufficiency) Let $Q\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ and define $C_Q$ as in (2.4). The characteristic function for the pair $(P,C_Q)$ is

\Delta_Q = (Y-NQ)D + (X+DQ)N = YD + XN = 1,

using the Bézout identity $XN+YD=1$. Since $\Delta_Q = 1$ is trivially a unit, internal stability follows from Theorem 2.1.
(Necessity) Suppose $C$ is any stabilizing controller. Write $C$ as a left fraction $C = \tilde X_c^{-1}\tilde Y_c$, with $\tilde X_c,\tilde Y_c\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ and $\tilde X_c$ a unit? No, we don't need $\tilde X_c$ to be a unit; we just need a coprime representation. From the stability theorem, $\tilde X_c D + \tilde Y_c N = U$ is a unit. Define

Q = (Y \tilde Y_c - X \tilde X_c) U^{-1}.

Using the Bézout identities, one verifies $Q\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ and $C = C_Q$. ∎

Corollary 2.1 (Affine parameterization of closed‑loop maps).
The closed‑loop transfer function from $r$ to $y$ is

T_{yr}(Q) = N (X + D Q).

It is affine in the Youla parameter $Q\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$. Many performance optimaization problems become convex in $Q$ precisely because of this affinity.

