2.5 The Two‑Plant Case: Strong Stabilization and the PIP

We now turn to the simultaneous stabilization of two plants $P_1$ and $P_2$. The problem reduces to finding a stable controller for a certain auxiliary plant.

Definition 2.5 (Strong stabilization). A plant $P$ is strongly stabilizable if there exists a controller $C\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ (a stable controller) that internally stabilizes it.

Theorem 2.3 (Two‑plant equivalence).
Assume that $P_1$ and $P_2$ are such that there exists a controller $C_0$ that stabilizes $P_1$. Then the set $\{P_1,P_2\}$ is simultaneously stabilizable if and only if the plant

\widetilde P = \frac{P_2 - P_1}{1 + P_1 C_0} \tag{2.5}

is strongly stabilizable (Vidyasagar, 1985, Section 5.5).

Proof outline. Using the Youla parameterization based on $P_1$ with parameter $Q$, any stabilizing controller for $P_1$ is $C_Q = C_0 + Q(1-P_1C_0)^{-1}$ (a common equivalent form). The condition that $C_Q$ also stabilizes $P_2$ forces $Q$ to strongly stabilize $\widetilde P$. The derivation involves straightforward algebraic manipulation in $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$. ∎

Thus the simultaneous stabilization of two plants is equivalent to the strong stabilization of one plant. This reduces the problem to a single‑plant test: the parity interlacing property.

Theorem 2.4 (Parity Interlacing Property – Youla, Bongiorno & Lu, 1974).
A real‑rational proper plant $P$ is strongly stabilizable if and only if it satisfies the Parity Interlacing Property (PIP):

Between every pair of real non‑negative zeros of $P$ (including infinity if $P$ is strictly proper), the number of real right‑half‑plane (RHP) poles is even.

Proof (necessity).
Let $C\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ stabilize $P$. Since $C$ is stable, it is analytic in $\overline{\mathbb{C}}^+$ and has no poles there. The Nyquist plot of $PC$ must not encircle $-1$ for stability, but the key comes from the interpolation conditions $C(z) = -1/P(z)$ at real RHP zeros $z\in[0,\infty]$. Because $C$ is real‑rational, continuous on $[0,\infty)$, and bounded at infinity, the values $C(z_i)$ must alternate signs if there is an odd number of real RHP poles between consecutive zeros; an even number repairs the alternation. The necessity of the PIP follows from a careful winding‑number argument (see Youla et al. 1974 for details).
Sufficiency is constructive: one can build a stable controller by interpolating with stable units. ∎

The PIP is a purely real‑axis condition. For two plants, simultaneous stabilizability is therefore completely characterized by this countable number of algebraic sign conditions.

