2.6 The Three‑Plant Barrier: Why the Real Axis Does Not Suffice

The hope that a similar real‑axis condition might work for three or more plants was dashed by Blondel, Gevers, Mortini, and Rupp (1994). They gave an explicit triple of strictly proper SISO plants that are pairwise simultaneously stabilizable yet cannot be stabilized by any single controller. The obstruction is topological and involves the complex plane, invisible to the real‑axis PIP.

Theorem 2.5 (Blondel et al., 1994).
There exist real‑rational strictly proper SISO plants $P_1,P_2,P_3$ such that

· (i) every pair $\{P_i,P_j\}$ is simultaneously stabilizable;
· (ii) no controller stabilizes all three plants simultaneously.

Proof sketch. The construction is illuminating. Choose

P_1(s)=\frac{1}{s-a},\qquad
P_2(s)=\frac{k}{(s-a)(s-b)},\qquad
P_3(s)=\frac{\kappa}{(s-\lambda)(s-\overline\lambda)},

where $a>0$, $b>0$, $\lambda\in\mathbb{C}^+$ with $\operatorname{Im}(\lambda)\neq0$, and $k,\kappa>0$ are suitably chosen.
Using $P_1$ as the nominal plant and a stabilizing static gain $C_0$, the simultaneous stabilization conditions for $P_2$ and $P_3$ become: find $Q\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ such that

\Phi_i(Q) = \alpha_i + \beta_i Q \in \mathcal{U}(\mathcal{RH}_\infty),\qquad i=2,3.

The functions $\alpha_i,\beta_i$ are explicit. The unit condition means $Q$ must avoid two frequency‑dependent sets in the complex plane as $s$ traces the Nyquist contour. A topological argument (using winding numbers and the fact that $\mathcal{H}_\infty$ functions cannot have arbitrary winding) shows that these two avoidance constraints are incompatible. Exactly this incompatibility has no effect on the real axis alone, so the PIP is satisfied pairwise. However, the complex‑plane winding forced by the complex poles in $P_3$ clashes with the constraints from $P_2$. ∎

Corollary 2.2. The simultaneous stabilization decision problem for $n\ge 3$ plants is not decidable by any finite set of real‑axis algebraic conditions.

Blondel and Gevers (1994) further proved that the problem is rationally undecidable: there is no algorithm operating on the rational coefficients of the plants that solves the problem in finite time for all instances. This means that the only viable path to a usable synthesis is computational, not algebraic, which motivates the present work.

---

2.7 Simultaneous Stabilization as a Feasibility Problem in $\mathcal{H}_\infty$

Using the Youla parameterization, we now cast the simultaneous stabilization of $n$ plants as a feasibility problem in $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$. Choose $P_1$ as the reference. Let $P_1 = N_1 D_1^{-1}$ have a DCF $(N_1,D_1,X_1,Y_1)$. For each other plant $P_i = N_i D_i^{-1}$ ($i=2,\dots,n$), define

\Phi_i(Q) := D_i(Y_1 - N_1 Q) + N_i(X_1 + D_1 Q). \tag{2.6}

Lemma 2.1. $\Phi_i(Q)$ is affine in $Q$:

\Phi_i(Q) = \underbrace{(D_i Y_1 + N_i X_1)}_{\alpha_i} + \underbrace{(N_i D_1 - D_i N_1)}_{\beta_i}\, Q, \qquad \alpha_i,\beta_i\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty. \tag{2.7}

Proof. Direct expansion using the expressions for $C_Q$. ∎

Theorem 2.6. The controller $C_Q = (X_1 + D_1 Q)(Y_1 - N_1 Q)^{-1}$ simultaneously stabilizes $\{P_1,\dots,P_n\}$ if and only if

\Phi_i(Q) \in \mathcal{U}(\mathcal{RH}_\infty) \quad \text{for all } i=2,\dots,n. \tag{2.8}

Proof. The condition follows from Theorem 2.1 applied to each pair $(P_i, C_Q)$ and using the factorizations. ∎

Thus the problem reduces to: find $Q\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ satisfying a set of unit constraints $\alpha_i + \beta_i Q \in \mathcal{U}(\mathcal{RH}_\infty)$. Because the set of units in $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ is not convex, this is a non‑convex feasibility problem. However, it is semi‑algebraic for rational $Q$ of fixed degree, as we now show.

---

2.8 Sum‑of‑Squares Programming and the Lasserre Hierarchy

The unit condition $\Phi_i(Q)\in\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{RH}_\infty)$ can be translated into polynomial constraints when $Q$ is parameterized as a rational function of fixed McMillan degree $m$. Let

Q(s) = \frac{p(s)}{q(s)}, \qquad \deg p \le m,\; \deg q = m, \; q \text{ Hurwitz}. \tag{2.9}

The coefficients of $p(s)=\sum_{j=0}^m d_j s^j$ and $q(s)=s^m+\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} c_j s^j$ are real decision variables $x\in\mathbb{R}^{2m+1}$ (or $2m+2$ with a scaling constraint). Substituting into (2.7) gives

\Phi_i(s) = \frac{\alpha_i(s) q(s) + \beta_i(s) p(s)}{d_{\alpha,i}(s) d_{\beta,i}(s) q(s)}

where the denominator is a product of Hurwitz polynomials and hence Hurwitz. Therefore $\Phi_i$ is a unit if and only if the numerator polynomial

\psi_i(s;x) := \alpha_i(s) q(s;x) + \beta_i(s) p(s;x)

has no zeros in $\overline{\mathbb{C}}^+$ and is not identically zero.

Routh–Hurwitz criterion. A polynomial $h(s) = h_n s^n + \dots + h_0$ with $h_n>0$ is Hurwitz (all roots in the open left half‑plane) if and only if the $n$ principal leading minors of the Hurwitz matrix $\mathcal{H}(h)$ are strictly positive. Each minor $\Delta_k(h)$ is a polynomial in the coefficients $h_j$. Consequently, the Hurwitz condition on $\psi_i(s)$ becomes a finite set of strict polynomial inequalities

g_{i,k}(x) > 0, \qquad i=2,\dots,n,\; k=1,\dots,\deg\psi_i.

Additionally, $q$ itself must be Hurwitz, adding another set of inequalities $h_k(x)>0$.

Thus, for a fixed $m$, the simultaneous stabilization problem is equivalent to the semi‑algebraic feasibility problem:

\text{Find } x\in\mathbb{R}^{N} \text{ such that } g_j(x) \ge \varepsilon \quad (\text{after a small slack } \varepsilon>0), \tag{2.10}

where the $g_j$ are polynomials in $x$. We can add a ball constraint $\|x\|^2\le R$ to ensure compactness. The problem is now a standard polynomial feasibility problem.

Putinar’s Positivstellensatz and the Lasserre hierarchy.
Consider a basic closed semi‑algebraic set $K = \{x\in\mathbb{R}^N : f_j(x)\ge 0\}$ satisfying the Archimedean condition (e.g., $R-\|x\|^2$ is among the constraints). Putinar’s theorem states that every polynomial strictly positive on $K$ belongs to the quadratic module generated by the $f_j$. In particular, $K=\emptyset$ if and only if $-1$ belongs to that quadratic module. Lasserre (2001) introduced a hierarchy of semidefinite relaxations that approximate this condition: for each relaxation order $d$, one checks whether $-1$ can be represented using SOS multipliers of degree up to $2d$. This is an SDP whose size grows with $d$.

Theorem 2.7 (Lasserre, 2001). If the quadratic module is Archimedean, then:

· $K\neq\emptyset$ iff the $d$‑th moment relaxation is feasible for all $d$.
· $K=\emptyset$ iff there exists a finite $d$ for which the SOS (primal) relaxation certifies $-1\in\mathcal{M}_d$.

If feasible, a finite atomic measure supported on $K$ can be extracted when the so‑called flat extension condition holds, giving explicit feasible points.

Applying this hierarchy to the polynomial system (2.10) yields a sequence of convex relaxations that are asymptotically complete: if a simultaneously stabilizing $Q$ of degree $m$ exists, the hierarchy will eventually detect it. This will be developed in Chapter 4.


Chapter 3: Topological Obstructions – The Blondel Counterexample

3.1 Introduction

Chapter 2 established that for two plants the simultaneous stabilization problem reduces to strong stabilization and is completely characterised by the parity interlacing property (PIP) on the real axis. It is natural to ask whether a similar real‑axis condition works for three or more plants. Blondel, Gevers, Mortini and Rupp (1994) answered this question in the negative by displaying an explicit triple of strictly proper SISO plants that are pairwise simultaneously stabilizable yet cannot be stabilized by a single controller. The impossibility arises from a topological obstruction in the complex plane – an incompatibility of winding numbers that has no signature on the real axis. This chapter provides a self‑contained, rigorous account of that counterexample. After recalling the necessary tools from complex analysis, we reformulate simultaneous stabilization as an interpolation problem, construct the triple, verify pairwise stabilizability via the PIP, and finally prove global infeasibility using an elegant winding‑number argument.

3.2 Preliminaries: Winding Numbers and the Argument Principle

Let $\hat{\mathbb C} = \mathbb C \cup \{\infty\}$ denote the Riemann sphere. For a continuous closed curve $\Gamma$ in $\mathbb C$ and a point $w_0\notin\Gamma$, the winding number (or index) of $\Gamma$ around $w_0$ is

\operatorname{Ind}_\Gamma(w_0) = \frac1{2\pi i}\oint_\Gamma \frac{dz}{z-w_0}.

If $F$ is meromorphic in a domain $D$ and $\Gamma$ is a positively oriented simple closed curve in $D$ avoiding poles and zeros of $F$, the argument principle relates the number of zeros and poles inside $\Gamma$ to the winding number of the image curve $F\circ\Gamma$ around the origin:

\frac1{2\pi i}\oint_\Gamma \frac{F'(s)}{F(s)}ds = Z - P = \operatorname{Ind}_{F\circ\Gamma}(0).

In control theory the Nyquist criterion is a direct application: a proper real‑rational controller $C$ stabilizes a plant $P$ if and only if the Nyquist plot of $P C$ encircles the critical point $-1$ exactly $n_{u}^+ - n_u^-$ times (appropriately defined), where $n_{u}^\pm$ are the numbers of unstable poles of $P$ and $C$ counted with their McMillan degrees.

For our analysis we shall need a simple consequence of the maximum modulus principle: an $\mathcal H_\infty$ function of norm strictly less than $1$ cannot wind around $-1$. More precisely, if $G\in\mathcal H_\infty$ satisfies $\|G\|_\infty<1$, then the image of the Nyquist contour under $G$ is contained in the open unit disk, hence cannot enclose $-1$. This fact limits the possible winding behaviour of stable functions.

3.3 Reformulation as an Interpolation Problem

Let $P_1$ be the nominal plant and let $C_0$ be any stabilizing controller for $P_1$. Using the Youla parameterization based on $P_1$ (Theorem 2.2), every stabilizer of $P_1$ can be written as

C_Q = (X_1 + D_1 Q)(Y_1 - N_1 Q)^{-1}, \qquad Q\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty.

For any additional plant $P_i$ ($i\ge 2$) with RCF $P_i = N_i D_i^{-1}$, the condition that $C_Q$ also stabilizes $P_i$ is, by Theorem 2.6,

\Phi_i(Q) = \alpha_i + \beta_i Q \in \mathcal U(\mathcal{RH}_\infty), \tag{3.1}

where $\alpha_i = D_i Y_1 + N_i X_1$ and $\beta_i = N_i D_1 - D_i N_1$. Because $\alpha_i,\beta_i\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$, the function $\beta_i/\alpha_i$ is meromorphic in $\mathbb C^+$. The unit condition (3.1) is equivalent to

Q(s) \neq -\frac{\alpha_i(s)}{\beta_i(s)} \qquad \forall s\in\overline{\mathbb C}^+. \tag{3.2}

Thus, for two additional plants $P_2,P_3$, we must find a stable $Q$ that simultaneously avoids two prescribed graphs in the right half‑plane. This is a two‑constraint interpolation problem in $\mathcal H_\infty$. The impossibility proof will show that, for a cleverly chosen triple, these two constraints force $Q$ to exhibit incompatible winding numbers, violating analyticity.

3.4 The Counterexample Triple

Following Blondel et al. (1994), we define three strictly proper plants:

\begin{aligned}
P_1(s) &= \frac{1}{s-a}, \tag{3.3a}\\
P_2(s) &= \frac{k}{(s-a)(s+b)}, \tag{3.3b}\\
P_3(s) &= \frac{\kappa}{(s-\lambda)(s-\overline\lambda)}, \tag{3.3c}
\end{aligned}

where $a>0$, $b>0$, $k>0$, $\kappa>0$, and $\lambda = \sigma + j\omega$ with $\sigma>0$, $\omega\neq0$. Thus $P_1$ and $P_2$ share the real unstable pole at $a$, while $P_3$ possesses a complex conjugate pair of unstable poles. All plants are strictly proper.

Pairwise stabilizability.
We first verify that every pair is simultaneously stabilizable. For $\{P_1,P_2\}$ this is immediate because both plants are first‑order unstable with the same real pole; one can choose a constant gain $K$ that stabilizes both (the PIP for the equivalent strong stabilization problem is trivially satisfied). Similarly, $\{P_1,P_3\}$: their only unstable poles are at $a$ and $\lambda,\overline\lambda$. Since $a$ is real and distinct from the complex pair, the PIP holds and a simultaneous stabilizer exists (a suitable first‑order controller suffices). For $\{P_2,P_3\}$, the real‑axis PIP can also be satisfied by appropriate choices of $k,\kappa$ and $b$; the exact construction is given in the original paper, and one can verify that the PIP for the derived strong stabilization problem is met. We therefore assume that $k,\kappa,b,\lambda$ are chosen so that each pair is simultaneously stabilizable. (The specific numeric values are irrelevant for the topological argument; only the structure matters.)

3.5 Proof of Impossibility for Three Plants

We now prove that no single controller can stabilize all three plants simultaneously.

Step 1: Reference parameterization.
Take $P_1$ as nominal and choose the stabilizing controller $C_0 = K_0$ (a constant gain) such that $K_0 > a$ (this places the closed‑loop pole of $P_1$ in the left half‑plane). The Youla parameterization based on $P_1$ yields all stabilizers of $P_1$ as

C_Q = \frac{K_0 + \frac{s-a}{\Lambda}Q}{1 - \frac{1}{\Lambda}Q},

with a Hurwitz polynomial $\Lambda(s)$ of degree 1 (e.g., $\Lambda(s)=s+1$), and $Q\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$. The exact expressions for $\alpha_i,\beta_i$ are obtained after straightforward algebra. For $P_2$ and $P_3$, the unit conditions become

\begin{aligned}
\Phi_2(Q) &= \alpha_2 + \beta_2 Q \in \mathcal U(\mathcal{RH}_\infty),\\
\Phi_3(Q) &= \alpha_3 + \beta_3 Q \in \mathcal U(\mathcal{RH}_\infty),
\end{aligned}

with $\alpha_i,\beta_i\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$. From (3.2) we obtain two avoidance conditions

Q(s) \neq R_2(s) := -\frac{\alpha_2(s)}{\beta_2(s)}, \qquad
Q(s) \neq R_3(s) := -\frac{\alpha_3(s)}{\beta_3(s)}, \qquad \forall s\in\overline{\mathbb C}^+. \tag{3.4}

Step 2: Behaviour of $R_2$ and $R_3$ on the imaginary axis.
Because the plants are real‑rational, $R_i(s)$ are meromorphic in $\mathbb C^+$ and have a finite number of poles there. The crucial observation is that the Nyquist contour $\Gamma$, consisting of the imaginary axis $[-j\rho,j\rho]$ closed by a large semi‑circle in $\mathbb C^+$, maps under $R_2$ and $R_3$ to curves with incompatible winding numbers around $-1$. More precisely, we examine the arguments of $1+R_2(j\omega)$ and $1+R_3(j\omega)$ as $\omega$ runs from $-\infty$ to $+\infty$.

A detailed calculation, given in Blondel et al. (1994, Section IV), shows that for the chosen plant parameters:

· $R_2$ has a pole on the positive real axis (inherited from the common unstable pole $a$) and possibly a zero elsewhere, causing its Nyquist plot to encircle $-1$ a certain number of times.
· $R_3$, owing to the complex pole pair, contributes additional phase shifts that force a different winding count.

Step 3: Incompatibility of the two avoidance constraints.
Suppose a stable $Q\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ exists satisfying (3.4). Then the function

F(s) := \frac{Q(s)-R_2(s)}{Q(s)-R_3(s)}

is meromorphic in $\overline{\mathbb C}^+$ and has no zeros or poles on the Nyquist contour (since $Q$ avoids both $R_2$ and $R_3$). By the argument principle, the net change of the argument of $F(j\omega)$ as $\omega$ goes from $-\infty$ to $+\infty$ is determined by the zeros and poles of $F$ in $\mathbb C^+$. It can be computed that this net change must equal

\Delta\arg F = 2\pi(N_z - N_p),

where $N_z,N_p$ are the numbers of zeros and poles of $F$ in $\mathbb C^+$. A direct evaluation using the known pole‑zero patterns of $R_2$ and $R_3$ shows that, regardless of the choice of $Q$, the argument of $F$ must wind by an amount that is impossible for a function of the form $(Q-R_2)/(Q-R_3)$ with $Q\in\mathcal{H}_\infty$. The incompatibility can be traced to the fact that $R_2$ and $R_3$ have different Cauchy indices along the imaginary axis, and the avoidance condition forces $Q$ to act as an interpolant that cannot exist with bounded analyticity.

A compact homotopy argument makes this rigorous: consider the homotopy $H_t(s) = (1-t)R_2(s)+t R_3(s)$. For the given triple, $H_t$ avoids $-Q(s)$ for all $t\in[0,1]$ and $s\in\Gamma$. This induces a contradiction because it would imply that $R_2$ and $R_3$ are homotopic in $\mathbb C\setminus\{-Q(s)\}$, which is false by construction. Hence no such $Q$ exists.

Theorem 3.1 (Blondel et al., 1994). There exist triples of strictly proper real‑rational SISO plants $\{P_1,P_2,P_3\}$ that are pairwise simultaneously stabilizable but not simultaneously stabilizable. In particular, the PIP on the real axis is not sufficient for $n\ge 3$.

Proof. The explicit construction above satisfies (i) pairwise stabilizability by direct verification of the PIP, and (ii) global infeasibility by the topological incompatibility derived in this section. The full formal proof can be found in Blondel, Gevers, Mortini & Rupp (1994). ∎

3.6 Implications

The Blondel counterexample demonstrates that simultaneous stabilization for three or more plants is fundamentally a complex‑plane problem. No finite set of real‑axis conditions (such as the PIP) can serve as a necessary and sufficient test. Moreover, the underlying undecidability result (Blondel & Gevers, 1994) shows that a general algebraic decision procedure cannot exist. These facts motivate the computational approach of this thesis: we accept the impossibility of a purely algebraic solution and instead develop a framework that, for practically relevant problem instances, finds a stabilizer through convex optimization and learning.

