Chapter 6: Complexity‑Theoretic Landscape

6.1 Introduction

The impossibility of a universal algebraic test for simultaneous stabilization of three or more plants (Chapter 3) raises the question: how hard is the decision problem, and are there structured subclasses for which efficient algorithms exist? This chapter surveys the known complexity results and organizes them to clarify the boundary between tractability and intractability. We present the rational undecidability theorem of Blondel and Gevers (1994), the NP‑hardness for bounded controller degree (Blondel & Tsitsiklis, 1997), and we identify four practically relevant subclasses that admit polynomial‑time algorithms. These results justify the computational philosophy of the thesis: worst‑case hardness necessitates the use of potentially exponential algorithms such as the Lasserre hierarchy, while the existence of tractable subclasses explains why a learning‑based method can succeed on the distribution of problems encountered in practice.

6.2 Rational Undecidability

The strongest negative result is that simultaneous stabilization of three SISO plants is undecidable over the field of rational numbers. Formally,

Theorem 6.1 (Blondel & Gevers, 1994). There exists no algorithm that, given the rational coefficients of three real‑rational strictly proper SISO plants, decides in a finite number of arithmetic operations whether a simultaneously stabilizing controller exists.

Proof sketch. The proof encodes a Diophantine equation (Hilbert’s tenth problem) into a family of plants parameterized by an integer $k$. The existence of an integer solution is equivalent to the simultaneous stabilizability of the constructed triple. Since there is no algorithm to decide Diophantine equations over $\mathbb Z$, the simultaneous stabilization problem is also undecidable over $\mathbb Q$. The construction uses the topological counterexample of Chapter 3 as a gadget that is activated exactly when a certain Diophantine condition is satisfied. For full details, see Blondel & Gevers (1994). ∎

This result explains why no finite list of algebraic conditions can provide a necessary and sufficient test: any such test would constitute a decision procedure, contradicting undecidability.

6.3 NP‑Hardness with Bounded Controller Order

If one restricts the controller to have a bounded McMillan degree, the problem becomes a semi‑algebraic feasibility question, but it remains computationally hard.

Theorem 6.2 (Blondel & Tsitsiklis, 1997). The decision problem “given three SISO plants and an integer $m$, does there exist a simultaneously stabilizing controller of McMillan degree $\le m$?” is NP‑hard.

Proof idea. The proof reduces 3‑SAT to simultaneous stabilization. Boolean variables are encoded by choices between two possible stabilizing controllers; clauses are represented by additional plants whose simultaneous stabilization forces the satisfaction of at least one literal. The Youla parameterization translates the encoding into interpolation constraints whose feasibility is equivalent to the original SAT instance. ∎

Thus, even with the controller order fixed, the worst‑case complexity is exponential (unless P = NP). This hardness provides theoretical justification for the SDP hierarchy, which in the worst case may need an exponential number of relaxations to converge.

6.4 Tractable Subclasses

Despite the general hardness, several special structures render the problem polynomial‑time solvable.

Class A: Plants sharing a common antistable part.
If all plants differ only by stable factors, i.e., $P_i = P_0 \cdot M_i$ where $P_0$ contains all the RHP poles and zeros and the $M_i$ are stable minimum‑phase transfer functions, then any controller that stabilizes $P_0$ also stabilizes all $P_i$. The problem reduces to stabilizing a single plant and is trivially polynomial‑time.

Class B: Real‑axis interlacing only.
When all unstable poles and zeros of the plants lie on the positive real axis (no complex RHP dynamics), the topological obstructions of Chapter 3 vanish. In this case the real‑axis PIP becomes necessary and sufficient for any number of plants. A constructive unit‑interpolation algorithm (Smith & Sondergeld, 1986) finds a stabilizer in polynomial time.

Class C: Simultaneous stabilization of two plants.
As shown in Chapter 2, this is equivalent to strong stabilization and is decided by the PIP test on a single derived plant, an $O(\deg P_1 + \deg P_2)$ algorithm.

Class D: Low‑order problems where the Lasserre hierarchy is exact at a fixed rank.
For many practical instances, the SDP hierarchy of Chapter 4 finds a solution at a small relaxation order $d$ that does not depend on the problem size. In such cases the SDP solves in polynomial time. While this is not a theoretical guarantee for all instances, it explains the empirical tractability observed in analogous polynomial optimization problems.

6.5 Implications for the Computational Framework

The complexity landscape provides a nuanced picture:

· General simultaneous stabilization is undecidable over the rationals and NP‑hard with bounded degree.
· Structured subclasses are polynomial‑time solvable.
· The Lasserre hierarchy is a complete algorithm for bounded degree; its worst‑case complexity is exponential, but its average‑case behaviour on practical instances is often polynomial.
· The learning‑based warm‑start of Chapter 5 exploits the fact that the distribution of plant triples encountered in practice is concentrated on the tractable subclasses, where a neural network can learn to approximate the mapping from plant parameters to Youla coefficients.

This marriage of worst‑case completeness and average‑case efficiency is the central achievement of the thesis.

6.6 Conclusion

The complexity‑theoretic results reinforce the motivation for the computational approach developed in earlier chapters. The known undecidability and NP‑hardness leave no hope for a universal algebraic solution; instead, they direct research towards algorithms that are rigorous when they succeed and practically efficient on the problems that actually arise. The framework of Chapters 4 and 5 is precisely such an algorithm.

