Appendix C: Strong Stabilization and the Parity Interlacing Property

Definition C.1. A plant $P$ is strongly stabilizable if there exists a stable controller $C\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ that internally stabilizes it.

Theorem C.2 (PIP, Youla et al. 1974).
A proper real‑rational plant $P$ is strongly stabilizable iff it satisfies the Parity Interlacing Property (PIP): between every pair of real non‑negative zeros of $P$ (including $+\infty$ if $P$ is strictly proper), the number of real RHP poles (counted with McMillan degree) is even.

Proof (necessity).
Assume $C\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ stabilizes $P$. $C$ is analytic in $\overline{\mathbb{C}}^+$ and continuous on $[0,\infty]\triangleq\mathbb{R}^+_\infty$. The Nyquist criterion requires that the image of the Nyquist contour under $PC$ encircles $-1$ exactly $n_u$ times (the number of RHP poles of $P$). Consider two consecutive non‑negative real zeros $z_1<z_2$ of $P$. At these points $P(z_i)=0$, so $C(z_i)$ must avoid $-1/P(z_i)=\infty$; indeed $C$ is finite because it is proper and stable. The key is that $C$, being real‑rational and bounded, must interpolate between $C(z_1)$ and $C(z_2)$. Between $z_1$ and $z_2$, the function $-1/P(s)$ crosses the real axis from $\infty$ to $\infty$ (with sign depending on the zero), and each real RHP pole between them flips the sign. For $C$ to avoid these infinite boundaries while remaining continuous, the number of such poles must be even; an odd number would force $C$ to cross the forbidden region, violating the Nyquist condition. A rigorous argument uses the intermediate value theorem applied to a continuous path on the Riemann sphere. ∎

(Sufficiency sketch.) If PIP holds, one can construct a stable controller by unit interpolation at the RHP poles. The classical proof (Youla et al., 1974) builds $C$ as a product of Blaschke products and all‑pass factors. Since this construction is technical but well‑established, we omit the details; they can be found in standard textbooks (Vidyasagar, 1985, Section 5.5). ∎

