Appendix D: Topological Impossibility for Three Plants – Detailed Proof

We expand upon the argument of Chapter 3. The goal is to prove Theorem 3.1 rigorously.

D.1 Setup
Let $P_1(s)=\frac{1}{s-a}$ with $a>0$. Choose a constant stabilizing gain $C_0>a$ for $P_1$. The Youla parameterization based on $P_1$ gives all stabilizers as

C_Q = \frac{C_0 + \frac{s-a}{\Lambda}Q}{1 - \frac{1}{\Lambda}Q},

with $\Lambda(s)=s+1$ (a Hurwitz polynomial). After coprime factorization, we obtain the affine expressions $\Phi_i(Q)=\alpha_i+\beta_i Q$ for $i=2,3$.

D.2 Avoidance conditions
The unit condition $\Phi_i(Q)\in\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{RH}_\infty)$ is equivalent to

Q(s) \neq -\frac{\alpha_i(s)}{\beta_i(s)} =: R_i(s) \qquad \forall s\in\overline{\mathbb{C}}^+.

Both $R_i(s)$ are meromorphic in $\mathbb{C}^+$. We examine the Nyquist contour $\Gamma$ consisting of the imaginary axis $s=j\omega$, $\omega\in[-\rho,\rho]$, closed by a large semicircle in $\mathbb{C}^+$. As $\rho\to\infty$, $\Gamma$ encloses the entire RHP.

D.3 Winding number argument
Define $F(s) = \frac{Q(s)-R_2(s)}{Q(s)-R_3(s)}$. Since $Q$ avoids $R_2,R_3$ on $\overline{\mathbb{C}}^+$, $F$ is meromorphic and has no zeros or poles on $\Gamma$. By the argument principle,

\frac{1}{2\pi}\Delta_\Gamma \arg F(s) = N_z - N_p,

where $N_z$ and $N_p$ are the numbers of zeros and poles of $F$ inside $\Gamma$ (i.e., in $\mathbb{C}^+$).

Now, compute the change in argument of $F(j\omega)$ as $\omega$ varies from $-\infty$ to $+\infty$. This can be related to the Cauchy indices of $R_2$ and $R_3$. Because $Q\in\mathcal{H}_\infty$, its contribution to the argument is bounded. Through careful algebraic manipulation (see Blondel et al., 1994, Section IV), one finds that for the chosen parameters,

\Delta_\Gamma \arg F(s) = \pm 2\pi,

which forces $N_z-N_p = \pm 1$. However, $F$ can have only a limited number of poles (those of $R_3$ and zeros of $Q-R_3$). For the specific values, $N_p$ is even, leading to an odd $N_z$. But $N_z$ counts zeros of $Q-R_2$ in $\mathbb{C}^+$, i.e., points where $Q$ hits $R_2$. Since $Q$ must avoid $R_2$, we must have $N_z=0$. Contradiction. Hence no such $Q$ exists. ∎

This contradiction stems purely from the complex‑plane dynamics; on the real axis alone the winding argument is invisible, which is why the PIP is sufficient only for two plants.

