Appendix E: Routh–Hurwitz Criterion and Polynomial Constraints

E.1 Hurwitz polynomial definition
A real polynomial $h(s)=h_n s^n + h_{n-1}s^{n-1}+\dots+h_0$ with $h_n>0$ is Hurwitz if all its roots have negative real parts.

E.2 Hurwitz matrix
Define the $n\times n$ matrix $\mathbf{H}(h)$ by

\mathbf{H}_{ij} = \begin{cases}
h_{2i-j}, & \text{if } 1\le 2i-j\le n,\\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

For example, for $n=3$:

\mathbf{H} = \begin{pmatrix}
h_2 & h_0 & 0\\
h_3 & h_1 & 0\\
0 & h_2 & h_0
\end{pmatrix}.

Theorem E.1 (Routh–Hurwitz). $h$ is Hurwitz iff all $n$ leading principal minors $\Delta_k = \det(\mathbf{H}_{1:k,1:k})$ are strictly positive.

Proof. See Gantmacher (1959, Chapter XV) or standard control textbooks. ∎

E.3 Application to simultaneous stabilization
For each polynomial $\psi_i(s;x)$ and $q(s;x)$, the Hurwitz determinants $\Delta_k$ are polynomial functions of the coefficient vector $x$. Thus strict positivity conditions become polynomial inequalities.

