Appendix F: Lasserre’s Moment‑SOS Hierarchy – Essentials

We provide a self‑contained summary of the key definitions and theorems used in Chapter 4.

F.1 Polynomial feasibility problem
Let $K=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^N : f_1(x)\ge 0,\dots,f_M(x)\ge 0\}$ be a basic closed semi‑algebraic set. Assume $K$ is compact (e.g., $R-\|x\|^2$ is among the constraints). The goal is to test whether $K\neq\emptyset$.

F.2 Moment matrix
Given a sequence $y=(y_\alpha)_{\alpha\in\mathbb{N}^N}$, the moment matrix of order $d$, $\mathbf{M}_d(y)$, is defined by

[\mathbf{M}_d(y)]_{\alpha,\beta} = y_{\alpha+\beta},\qquad |\alpha|,|\beta|\le d.

Localizing matrix w.r.t. a polynomial $f(x)=\sum_\gamma f_\gamma x^\gamma$ of degree $d_f$:

[\mathbf{M}_d(f\,y)]_{\alpha,\beta} = \sum_\gamma f_\gamma \; y_{\alpha+\beta+\gamma},\qquad |\alpha|,|\beta|\le d - \lceil d_f/2\rceil.

F.3 Putinar’s Positivstellensatz
If the quadratic module generated by $\{f_j\}$ is Archimedean (always true when the ball constraint is present), then every polynomial strictly positive on $K$ can be written as $\sigma_0+\sum_j \sigma_j f_j$ with $\sigma_j$ sums of squares (SOS) of polynomials.

F.4 Lasserre’s hierarchy
For $d\in\mathbb{N}$, the primal SOS relaxation checks whether $-1$ can be expressed as in Putinar’s theorem with degrees bounded by $2d$. Equivalent to an SDP feasibility problem.
The dual moment relaxation is:

\begin{aligned}
\text{find } & y \text{ s.t. } \\
& \mathbf{M}_d(y) \succeq 0,\\
& \mathbf{M}_{d-d_j}(f_j y) \succeq 0,\; j=1,\dots,M,\\
& y_0 = 1,
\end{aligned}

where $d_j=\lceil\deg f_j/2\rceil$.

Theorem F.1 (Lasserre 2001). If $K$ is Archimedean,

· $K\neq\emptyset$ iff the moment relaxation is feasible for all $d$.
· $K=\emptyset$ iff $\exists d$ such that $-1\in\mathcal{M}_d$.
· If feasible and flat extension condition holds at some $d$, then the truncation corresponds to a finite atomic measure on $K$, and extracting the support yields feasible points.

F.5 SOS ⇔ SDP
A polynomial $p(x)$ of degree $2d$ is SOS iff there exists a positive semidefinite matrix $Q$ such that $p(x)=z(x)^\top Q z(x)$, where $z(x)$ is the vector of monomials up to degree $d$. This equivalence transforms SOS feasibility into a semidefinite program.

