Appendix G: Generalization Bounds via Rademacher Complexity

G.1 Definitions
For a function class $\mathcal{F}$ mapping inputs $\xi$ to $\mathbb{R}$, the empirical Rademacher complexity on a sample $S=(\xi_1,\dots,\xi_T)$ is

\widehat{\mathfrak{R}}_S(\mathcal{F}) = \frac1T \mathbb{E}_\sigma \left[\sup_{f\in\mathcal{F}} \sum_{t=1}^T \sigma_t f(\xi_t) \right],

where $\sigma_t$ are independent Rademacher variables $\operatorname{Unif}\{-1,+1\}$. The population version is $\mathfrak{R}_T(\mathcal{F})=\mathbb{E}_S[\widehat{\mathfrak{R}}_S(\mathcal{F})]$.

G.2 Uniform convergence bound
Let $\ell:\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Y}\to[0,C]$ be a bounded loss, $\mathcal{H}$ a hypothesis class. For any $\delta\in(0,1)$, with probability $>1-\delta$ over $S$ of size $T$,

\forall h\in\mathcal{H},\; \mathbb{E}[\ell(h(\xi),y)] \le \frac1T\sum_{t=1}^T \ell(h(\xi_t),y_t) + 2\mathfrak{R}_T(\ell\circ\mathcal{H}) + 3C\sqrt{\frac{\ln(2/\delta)}{2T}}.

G.3 Rademacher complexity of deep MLPs
Golowich, Rakhlin & Shamir (2018) showed that for a feedforward network $f_\theta$ with depth $L$, width $W$, ReLU/GELU activations, and weight matrices bounded in Frobenius norm by $B_F$, the empirical Rademacher complexity satisfies

\widehat{\mathfrak{R}}_S(\mathcal{F}) \le \frac{B_F (2\sqrt{L} + 1) \prod_{i=1}^L \|W_i\|_F}{\sqrt{T}} \le \frac{c B_\theta W^{L/2}}{\sqrt{T}}.

This bound is used in Theorem 5.1.

G.4 Application to YPN
In Chapter 5, the loss $\ell(\hat x, x^*)=\|\hat x-x^*\|^2$ is $\rho$-Lipschitz with $\rho$ depending on the maximum coefficient magnitude. Composing with the network yields the stated generalization bound.

