Appendix H: Additional Proofs and Technical Lemmas

H.1 Bézout identity for polynomials
If $a(s),b(s)$ are coprime real polynomials, there exist polynomials $x(s),y(s)$ such that $xa+yb=1$. This follows from the extended Euclidean algorithm. Dividing by a Hurwitz $\Lambda$ gives the $\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ version.

H.2 Affine form of $\Phi_i(Q)$
From $C_Q=(X_1+D_1Q)(Y_1-N_1Q)^{-1}$ and $P_i=N_i D_i^{-1}$, the characteristic function for $(P_i,C_Q)$ is

\begin{aligned}
\Phi_i(Q) &= D_i(Y_1-N_1Q) + N_i (X_1+D_1Q)\\
&= D_i Y_1 + N_i X_1 + (N_i D_1 - D_i N_1) Q.
\end{aligned}

Thus $\Phi_i(Q)=\alpha_i+\beta_i Q$ with $\alpha_i,\beta_i\in\mathcal{RH}_\infty$ as claimed.

H.3 Flat extension condition (Curto & Fialkow, 1996)
If the rank of $\mathbf{M}_d(y)$ equals the rank of $\mathbf{M}_{d-1}(y)$, then $y$ admits a unique representing measure consisting of $r=\operatorname{rank}$ atoms. The support of that measure gives feasible points of the polynomial system. This result justifies the extraction step in the SDP hierarchy.

H.4 Undecidability reduction (Blondel & Gevers)
The reduction from Hilbert’s tenth problem uses a parametric family of plants whose simultaneous stabilizability is equivalent to the existence of an integer solution to a Diophantine equation. The construction is highly technical; the interested reader is referred to the original paper.

Appendix I: Algorithms

This appendix collects the principal algorithms developed in the thesis. All algorithms are presented as conceptual specifications; their theoretical properties (correctness, convergence, complexity) are proved in Chapters 2–5, and no computer implementation has yet been executed. The pseudocode uses standard mathematical notation and is intended to be sufficiently detailed for a reader to implement the procedures in a language such as MATLAB or Python with appropriate symbolic and SDP toolboxes.

